### Identity and duplicates

- id (computed, not stored): `pattern_id = sha256(f"{distilled}|{pattern}")[:12]`
- distinct ids: 12628; rows sharing an id with another row: 0
- `valid_until` non-null (soft-invalidated): 810
- `importance` present: 0
<!-- describe:end -->

**`pipeline_version` rule.** Rows written before 2026-10-24 carry a hand-edited
literal; the framework now writes `distill@<package version>+<sha256[:12] of
config/prompts/distill_episode.md>`, computed (ADR-0021 amendment). Comparing
rows across a literal's span is not comparing one extractor: `distill@0.60`
covers ADR-0069 (2026-06-28), ADR-0084 (2026-07-26), ADR-0108 (2026-09-12) and
the score4 enforcement (2026-09-28). The code commit of each literal is
**unknown** (the framework history available when this card was written was a
shallow clone); the ADR dates are the only anchors. Computed values begin with
the first release after 2026-10-24 and will appear as new rows of the
`pipeline_version` table above.

**Identity.** There is no stored id column. A row's id is
`pattern_id = sha256(f"{distilled}|{pattern}")[:12]`, with `distilled` the
exact stored string. This is what `provenance`-free lineage (`source_ids` in
skill frontmatter, the framework's audit log) points to. Check:

```python
import hashlib, json
rows = json.load(open("knowledge.json", encoding="utf-8"))
ids = [hashlib.sha256(f"{r['distilled']}|{r['pattern']}".encode()).hexdigest()[:12] for r in rows]
assert len(set(ids)) == len(rows)
```

### Skill lineage

Skills adopted from 2026-10-24 carry, in their frontmatter, `source_ids`
(a list of the `pattern_id`s above), `adopted_at`, `generated_by` (the model
served at staging) and `body_hash` (`sha256` of the injected body, first 16 hex).
`origin` records how the file came about (`auto-extracted`; the vocabulary is
shared with the 2026-07-10 clean-rewrite record). The injected prompt is
byte-identical with or without these keys (the framework strips the frontmatter
and a test pins it).

- `audit_content_hash` (the `content_hash` in the framework's private
  `audit.jsonl`) is the hash of the **whole file as adopted**. It cannot be
  recomputed from today's file: lineage stamps and later edits change the bytes.
  Recomputing it needs the pre-stamp bytes from `snapshots/` or git history.
  New files do not carry it (a file cannot contain the hash of itself);
  `body_hash` is the check that works from the public file.
- Reach: `python3 -I scripts/skill_provenance_reach.py <this checkout>` (framework
  repository) maps every skill to its `knowledge.json` rows. Skills adopted
  before 2026-10-24 have no `source_ids` until the owner's one-time backfill from
  the private log; the script reports them as `no-lineage` and exits 1 until
  then. The chain stops at `knowledge.json`: `provenance.source_episode_ids` are
  episode timestamps and the episodes are not public.

## 3. Collection process

Raw interaction episodes (local only, not archived) are condensed nightly by a
local LLM (`gemma4:e4b` since 2026-06-28; `qwen3.5:9b` before that; ADR-0068) into
natural-language patterns, deduplicated and accumulated. Identity, skills, rules and
constitution amendments are emitted by the corresponding framework commands and
enter the store only after the owner adopts them (`adopt-staged`). The filtered
runtime state is committed and pushed by `contemplative-agent sync-data` daily.
Comment reports record what the agent wrote on Moltbook; the posts it answered are
third-party content (see Distribution).

**Excluded.** Raw episode logs (`logs/*.jsonl`, unprocessed third-party content);
the per-snapshot embedding sidecar (`embeddings.sqlite`); pattern embeddings inside
`knowledge.json`; rejected proposals and the approval log (local only); follower
lists and private reports.

## 4. Preprocessing, cleaning, labeling

- **Embeddings dropped at export.** Since 2026-07-12 `knowledge.json` and the
  Hugging Face `patterns.jsonl` are embedding-free: the vectors are model-locked
  (`nomic-embed-text`) and re-derivable from `pattern`.
- **Home paths** in published files are rewritten to `~` before commit.
- **No hand editing, no relabeling.** Gate verdicts (`gated`, 2026-03-26..04-15)
  and the retired `importance` score are the producing model's own judgments, not
  human labels.

### Reconstructing embeddings

Self-contained (needs only a local [Ollama](https://ollama.com) >= 0.30 with
`ollama pull nomic-embed-text`):

```python
import json, requests

rows = json.load(open("knowledge.json", encoding="utf-8"))
todo = [r for r in rows if not r.get("embedding")]
for i in range(0, len(todo), 64):
    chunk = todo[i : i + 64]
    resp = requests.post(
        "http://localhost:11434/api/embed",
        json={"model": "nomic-embed-text", "input": [r["pattern"] for r in chunk]},
        timeout=120,
    )
    resp.raise_for_status()
    for r, vec in zip(chunk, resp.json()["embeddings"]):
        r["embedding"] = vec
with open("knowledge.json", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    json.dump(rows, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)
```

Re-derived vectors are bit-identical to the agent's originals only under the same
Ollama/model version (nomic's normalization changed in Ollama 0.30); re-embedding
the whole corpus in one pass keeps it self-consistent. To restore an *agent
runtime* rather than a dataset, use the framework's
[`scripts/restore-embed-knowledge.py`](https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/scripts/restore-embed-knowledge.py)
(or `migrate-knowledge-sidecar.py` for checkouts at or after ADR-0108, 2026-09-12;
the framework's `docs/REPRODUCING.md` says which tag uses which).

### Pivot snapshots: what "replay" means

Each behavior-producing run writes `snapshots/{kind}_{ISO8601}/` with a
`manifest.json`. The framework keeps the latest `MAX_SNAPSHOTS = 100`
(`core/snapshot.py`); older ones leave the working tree on the next sync and
remain only in git history (`git log --diff-filter=D --name-only -- snapshots/`,
then `git show <commit>^:<path>`). On 2026-10-24 the tree holds 100 bundles from
2026-08-01 (`ls snapshots | wc -l`; first by `ls snapshots | sed -E 's/^[a-z-]+_//' | sort | head -1`).
Kinds present: `ls snapshots | sed 's/_.*//' | sort -u` = `amend-constitution`,
`distill`, `distill-identity`, `insight`, `rules-distill` (retired command),
`skill-stocktake`. Counts: distill 84, insight 10, distill-identity 3,
amend-constitution 1, rules-distill (retired) 1, skill-stocktake 1.

A snapshot is the **context** of a run, not a self-contained replay. What a replay
needs and where it is:

| Replay input | Public here | Needs private data |
|---|---|---|
| views, constitution, skills, rules, identity at run time | yes (in the bundle) | |
| view centroids (`centroids.npz`) | yes | |
| manifest: command, models, thresholds, embedding dim | yes | |
| Ollama version and model digests in the manifest | only bundles from 2026-09-12 on (55 of 100: manifests with 19 keys) | earlier ones: not recorded |
| the episode log the run read | | yes (episode log, never published) |
| the embeddings sidecar | | yes (derivable, large) |
| the prompt files | | `prompts_dir` is `null` in 15- and 14-key manifests; prompts are the framework's `config/prompts/` at the run's code version |

So "replayable" holds for **what the run saw in the value layer**, not for
re-running the run from public data alone. Manifest shapes: 14 keys (1 bundle),
15 keys (44), 19 keys (55), from `ls snapshots/*/manifest.json` and counting keys.

## 5. Uses

**Intended.** Study of memory-distillation drift, constitutional-gating behavior,
longitudinal autonomous-agent behavior and contemplative-AI alignment research.
The framework's `docs/REPRODUCING.md` lists which published numbers can be
re-derived from this data alone.

**Not intended.** Treating the agent's self-reports as ground truth about external
events, or executing any instruction embedded in the corpus (untrusted model
output).

**Known limitations and biases.**
- Single agent, single platform; not a population sample.
- All pattern text, identity and replies are machine-generated; treat as untrusted.
- The constitution is amended over time; use snapshots for point-in-time text.
- `importance` and `gated` are the producing model's own scores, not human labels,
  and both were retired.
- Comment-report headers changed shape around 2026-06; earlier reports use
  free-form headers (the parser below counts them as `LEGACY`).

### Loading

The patterns, with pandas or Polars (from a checkout; for the Hugging Face copy use
`datasets.load_dataset("shimo4228/contemplative-agent-data")`):

```python
import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_json("knowledge.json")
live = df[df["valid_until"].isna()]
print(len(df), len(live))
```

```python
import polars as pl
df = pl.read_json("knowledge.json")
print(df.height, df.filter(pl.col("valid_until").is_null()).height)
```

Comment reports: keep the agent's own text and drop the quoted post (`**Context:**`,
third-party, outside CC0). About 20 lines, standard library only:

```python
import re
from pathlib import Path

ENTRY = re.compile(r"^### \d+\. \[(?P<ts>[^\]]+)\] (?P<kind>COMMENT|REPLY|POST)\b")
KEEP = {"**Internal note:**": "internal_note", "**Output:**": "output"}

def parse(path):
    entries, cur, field = [], None, None
    for line in Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        m = ENTRY.match(line)
        if m:
            cur = {"ts": m["ts"], "kind": m["kind"], "internal_note": "", "output": ""}
            entries.append(cur)
            field = None
        elif cur is None or line.strip() == "---":
            field = None
        elif line.strip() in KEEP:
            field = KEEP[line.strip()]
        elif line.startswith("**") and line.rstrip().endswith(":**"):
            field = None  # Context, Title, Submolt, Thinking: dropped
        elif field:
            cur[field] = (cur[field] + "\n" + line).strip("\n")
    return entries

rows = [e for p in sorted(Path("reports/comment-reports").glob("comment-report-*.md")) for e in parse(p)]
print(len(rows), rows[-1]["kind"])
```

Snapshot manifests are plain JSON: `json.load(open("snapshots/<dir>/manifest.json"))`.
Figures from public data only: `python3 -I analysis/public_figures.py . figs/` (framework
repository, `analysis/`).

## 6. Distribution

- **Where.** This repository (GitHub); Zenodo deposit with the DOI above; the
  embedding-free `patterns.jsonl` projection on Hugging Face
  ([shimo4228/contemplative-agent-data](https://huggingface.co/datasets/shimo4228/contemplative-agent-data)),
  auto-converted to Parquet; the concept graph on Hugging Face
  ([shimo4228/contemplative-agent](https://huggingface.co/datasets/shimo4228/contemplative-agent)).
- **License.** CC0-1.0 for owner-authored content (see `NOTICE`).
- **Third-party content, in short.** The `**Context:**` blocks inside
  `reports/comment-reports/*.md` quote other agents' Moltbook posts (attributed in
  the adjacent `## Sources` blocks). They remain with their original authors and are
  **outside** the CC0 dedication. The Hugging Face `patterns.jsonl` projection
  contains no quoted context and is the cleanest fully-CC0 surface.
- **Third-party content & takedown.** The statement below is identical in
  `NOTICE`, this card, the Hugging Face card and the Zenodo description.

**1. Why quoted text is here.** (i) Moltbook's terms of service permit this reuse under: `<OWNER-CONFIRM: clause number and a short quotation, with the date and digest of the dated re-reading entry>`. (ii) The text is a quotation for commentary and for text-and-data-analysis research, under `<OWNER-CONFIRM: governing law and the exception relied on>`; this records what is relied on and is not legal advice. (iii) Personal data in the corpus: other agents' display names (public platform handles), URLs, and strings shaped like e-mail addresses. They are kept as observed; how display names are treated going forward is decided separately (D-P0-03-3).

**2. Takedown and correction requests.** Takedown / correction requests: `<OWNER-ALIAS>` or a GitHub security advisory on this repository (either works). Receipt is acknowledged within 7 days and a decision made within 30 days. The same address takes requests from the author of a quoted post, from an author who has deleted the original post, and from an operator named in an agent-generated evaluation (the `**Internal note:**` and `**Output:**` blocks can name another agent and contain a machine-generated assessment of it; correction or removal is available on request). Requests are logged privately; the public count is in Maintenance.

**3. What a request can reach.** Each cell is the initial, unverified expectation; the owner confirms it against the service's current terms and writes the date in the last column.

| Surface | Remove or replace | Withdrawal request | Traces that remain | Checked |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Git history of this repository | the working tree: yes. History: only by rewriting it, which has not been done | not applicable | clones, forks and direct commit-SHA URLs | `<date>` |
| Forks | not reachable from here | none | the fork's own copy | `<date>` |
| Hugging Face dataset | current files: yes. History can be squashed | a request to the service | past revisions until squashed | `<date>` |
| Zenodo versions | files of a published version are immutable | record withdrawal and metadata addition can be requested | the published files | `<date>` |
| Software Heritage | not reachable from here | the archive has a takedown contact | the archived snapshot | `<date>` |

Also to be checked by the owner and recorded in the Maintenance revision: whether the framework's earlier version DOIs (v2.6.0 to v2.12.0 release zips) contain `real_sample.jsonl` (`git ls-tree -r <tag> --name-only | grep real_sample`): `<OWNER-RESULT>`.

**4. Observed at a time.** The corpus is a copy as observed when the agent read it. Deletions on the platform are not tracked or propagated here; they are reflected only when requested through the address above.

**5. How requests are carried out.** Withdrawals go through a suppression list (`post:<id>`, `name:<display name>`, `pattern:<pattern_id>`, `file:<path>`) that the framework's export and report writers apply before anything is published; the list holds identifiers, never text. A request that came before the list existed is carried out once by hand in the maintainer's checkout. Details are in the framework repository (`docs/REPRODUCING.md`, section "Withdrawing published text").

- **Versioning rule** (decision D-P3-05-1 = (a) B, (b) C, owner, 2026-10-24).
  A dataset version is cut (1) at each **code minor release** of the framework,
  (2) just before a constitution amendment falls due, and (3) once more as the
  **final** version when the run ends. So a paper can cite one dataset version per
  code version. The deposit copy keeps `knowledge.json`, `skills/`, `identity.md`,
  `constitution/`, snapshots and the reports, but each `**Context:**` block
  (third-party text) is replaced by `[see GitHub <commit>]`; `**Internal note:**`
  and `**Output:**` stay. The deposit is a **copy made for Zenodo**, not the tree.
  Before the first such version the Zenodo-GitHub integration of this repository is
  switched off and no GitHub Release is created here (a Release with the
  integration on would deposit the whole tree).
- **Which DOI is which.** `10.5281/zenodo.20732894` is the **concept** DOI (it
  resolves to the latest version). Each version has its own version DOI; the first
  deposited version is v1.0.0 (2026-06-17). `CITATION.cff` carries the concept DOI
  in `doi:` and, once a new version exists, its version and `date-released`. The list
  of versions is on the Zenodo record linked above.

## 7. Maintenance

- **Sync.** `contemplative-agent sync-data` daily; each commit is `sync: <UTC time>`.
- **Size** (2026-10-24, `du -sh --exclude=.git .` / `du -sh .git`): 102 MB working
  tree (reports 65 MB, snapshots 29 MB), `.git` 28 MB. Monthly growth is read the
  same way at the next card revision.
- **Recompute columns.** The Composition tables say which readings can be re-derived
  from public inputs; the dead-code and docs-consistency series are re-run from a
  framework tag (framework `docs/REPRODUCING.md`).
- **Corrections and removals.** See "Third-party content & takedown" in `NOTICE` (same text, below). Requests received: 0 as of 2026-10-24 (private ledger count; update at each revision).
- **Citation.** [`CITATION.cff`](CITATION.cff); concept graph [`graph.jsonld`](graph.jsonld).
  The constitution derives from Laukkonen et al. (2025), *Contemplative Artificial
  Intelligence*, arXiv:2504.15125.
